WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.516 --> 00:00:11.780
Mom popped her head into my room.

2
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.682
"Your room is clean!"

3
00:00:15.702 --> 00:00:19.383
"I'm building my computer," I told her.

4
00:00:19.403 --> 00:00:23.265
"You have to build a computer in a clean space.

5
00:00:24.345 --> 00:00:25.606
No dust or cat hair."

6
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:30.228
Dad came in and sat on my bed.

7
00:00:32.256 --> 00:00:35.358
He looked impressed when he saw the computer parts spread out on my desk.

8
00:00:35.378 --> 00:00:38.940
"So what do you do first?"

9
00:00:41.401 --> 00:00:42.822
"This is the power supply."

10
00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:46.584
I held up a black box with cords running out.

11
00:00:46.604 --> 00:00:51.627
"First I'll put it in the computer's case so I can plug the computer in."

12
00:00:51.647 --> 00:00:54.269
"Be careful!"

13
00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:55.249
said Mom.

14
00:00:55.269 --> 00:01:02.317
"I'll keep everything unplugged while I'm building," I said as I screwed the power supply into the case.

15
00:01:03.877 --> 00:01:05.718
"Next I have to work with the motherboard."

16
00:01:07.778 --> 00:01:08.759
I held up the board.

17
00:01:10.599 --> 00:01:11.319
Mom chuckled.

18
00:01:11.339 --> 00:01:15.681
"It looks more like a 'robot' board than a 'mother' board."

19
00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:20.282
"A motherboard is like the computer's nervous system.

20
00:01:22.503 --> 00:01:26.364
Other parts plug into it, and the motherboard lets the parts communicate.

21
00:01:27.788 --> 00:01:31.850
The motherboard makes sure the information in the computer gets to where it needs to go."

22
00:01:33.731 --> 00:01:39.913
"Your mother makes sure everyone in our family gets to where they need to go," said Dad.

23
00:01:42.094 --> 00:01:42.854
Mom laughed.

24
00:01:45.856 --> 00:01:47.757
I picked up a small square package.

25
00:01:47.777 --> 00:01:52.359
"This is the central processing unit, or CPU.

26
00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:55.460
The CPU does all of the computer's work.

27
00:01:57.512 --> 00:01:58.873
It's like the computer's brain."

28
00:01:58.893 --> 00:02:02.777
"That's a pretty thin brain!"

29
00:02:03.197 --> 00:02:03.777
said Dad.

30
00:02:03.797 --> 00:02:06.600
"How can it remember anything?"

31
00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:12.865
"The CPU isn't exactly like a person's brain," I explained.

32
00:02:12.885 --> 00:02:19.150
"It does the thinking for the computer, but it doesn't actually store information.

33
00:02:20.932 --> 00:02:22.953
There are two other pieces of hardware for that."

34
00:02:24.655 --> 00:02:25.996
I pointed to a metal box.

35
00:02:27.884 --> 00:02:30.686
"The hard drive is where the computer stores all of its information.

36
00:02:32.327 --> 00:02:35.530
When you save something, it's stored on the hard drive."

37
00:02:37.671 --> 00:02:39.472
Then I held up a rectangular piece.

38
00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:42.234
"This is RAM.

39
00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.976
That stands for random access memory.

40
00:02:46.518 --> 00:02:49.760
RAM is where the computer puts information while it's working on it.

41
00:02:51.481 --> 00:02:55.204
I'm putting in extra RAM so I can work on several things at the same time."

42
00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.502
"I could use extra memory!"

43
00:03:01.002 --> 00:03:01.723
Dad laughed.

44
00:03:03.604 --> 00:03:06.345
I attached the RAM and CPU to the motherboard.

45
00:03:10.147 --> 00:03:12.569
Then I carefully placed the motherboard in the case.

46
00:03:16.711 --> 00:03:17.812
the computer ready?"

47
00:03:18.112 --> 00:03:18.692
asked Mom.

48
00:03:20.473 --> 00:03:21.034
"Not yet."

49
00:03:22.755 --> 00:03:24.576
I pointed out spots on the motherboard.

50
00:03:27.008 --> 00:03:30.129
"These places are where you add parts for sound and graphics.

51
00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.410
I got a good graphics card for playing games."

52
00:03:39.572 --> 00:03:43.233
I put the remaining parts into place and connected my monitor to the computer.

53
00:03:47.094 --> 00:03:50.395
plug it in now," I said, crossing my fingers for luck.

54
00:03:51.995 --> 00:03:55.496
Dad plugged the computer into a power strip, and I switched it on.

55
00:03:56.996 --> 00:04:00.479
We heard the computer start up, and then numbers scrolled up the screen.

56
00:04:02.221 --> 00:04:06.484
Mom and Dad cheered and I let out a sigh of relief.

57
00:04:10.047 --> 00:04:11.528
you surf the internet now?"

58
00:04:11.969 --> 00:04:12.609
Mom asked.

59
00:04:14.611 --> 00:04:15.111
"Not yet.

60
00:04:15.772 --> 00:04:17.673
This is just the hardware," I said.

61
00:04:17.693 --> 00:04:25.540
"I still have to install software—the programs and code that tell the computer what to do."

62
00:04:27.232 --> 00:04:28.594
"Can you write code too?"

63
00:04:29.055 --> 00:04:30.357
asked Dad teasingly.

64
00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:32.821
I smiled.

65
00:04:33.542 --> 00:04:34.864
"That's my next project."
